1	/*
2	 * keylife.c -- external ptrace observer for the keygen temporary-key
3	 * lifecycle regression tests.
4	 *
5	 * It is NOT an LD_PRELOAD shim and changes nothing in the observed program:
6	 * the test harness forks, the child PTRACE_TRACEME's itself, redirects its
7	 * stdout/stderr to capture files, and execs the real wrapfile binary; this
8	 * program watches from outside. That lets a regression test prove
9	 * properties no black-box observation can -- deleting the output file,
10	 * keeping the key out of stdout/stderr, and exiting 0/1 are all compatible
11	 * with the 32 secret bytes never being wiped from the stack (the whole
12	 * process image vanishes on exit either way, and a stack frame may be
13	 * reused after the function returns). The memory holding the delivered
14	 * bytes has to be inspected *while the operation is still in flight*.
15	 *
16	 * What is observed for one `wrapfile keygen <path>` run
17	 * ----------------------------------------------------
18	 * The key draw (getrandom(2); the getrandom crate issues it with inline
19	 * assembly, and under the tests the randtrap shim emulates it with a
20	 * SECCOMP_RET_TRAP SIGSYS handler), the key-file open/write/fsync/close
21	 * sequence, the user-space instruction at which the 32-byte buffer is
22	 * overwritten, and the first stdout/stderr write after that:
23	 *
24	 *   1. The 32-byte draw really is delivered into one buffer (address P):
25	 *      after the fill completes, [P, P+32) holds 32 *non-zero* bytes (the
26	 *      deterministic randtrap pattern, 0x80.. by draw index). A wipe that
27	 *      "succeeded" on an all-zero buffer would prove nothing.
28	 *   2. On the save path the same bytes are still there every time the key
29	 *      bytes are offered to write(2) on the key fd -- zeroing cannot have
30	 *      happened before/while saving (which would turn the saved file into
31	 *      an all-zero or truncated key) -- and they are still there when
32	 *      close(2) on the key fd returns on the success path.
33	 *   3. After the watch arm point the observer single-steps the guest and
34	 *      requires the whole 32-byte buffer to become zero within a bounded
35	 *      number of user instructions -- a tight loop right there, not a later
36	 *      frame reuse -- and before the program performs its first write(2) to
37	 *      stdout or stderr. A missing wipe, a wipe the optimizer deleted (the
38	 *      guest is inspected under both debug and release builds), a wipe
39	 *      covering only part of the buffer, and a wipe placed before the save
40	 *      are all FAILs.
41	 *
42	 * Watch arm points: success  -> the key fd's close(2) exit;
43	 *                   randfail -> the emulated getrandom error visible after
44	 *                               SIGSYS handler rt_sigreturn;
45	 *                   writefail-> the key fd write(2) exit carrying EIO;
46	 *                   closefail-> the key fd's close(2) exit. The permfail
47	 *                               shim reports the close error in user space
48	 *                               after really releasing the descriptor, so
49	 *                               the close syscall itself succeeds; the
50	 *                               failed save then reaches abort_save, whose
51	 *                               unlinkat(2) of this run's file is observed
52	 *                               while stepping and must still see the full
53	 *                               key (when the shim also refuses the unlink
54	 *                               in user space no unlinkat syscall happens,
55	 *                               so that event is simply absent).
56	 *
57	 * Partial-draw failure needs one extra trick. When the random source dries
58	 * up after `held` (< 32) bytes, the remaining 32-held slots have never been
59	 * written by the source and stay zero from the array's zero initialization,
60	 * so inspecting buffer contents alone cannot tell a full-buffer wipe from a
61	 * wipe covering only the delivered prefix. Immediately after observing the
62	 * emulated getrandom failure (while the guest is stopped), the observer
63	 * writes a non-zero sentinel pattern into [P+held, P+32) -- memory the
64	 * product never reads on the error path (getrandom already returned Err and
65	 * the command only returns from there). The wipe must erase the sentinel
66	 * too.
67	 *
68	 * Output (stdout) is one `KEY=VALUE` event per line for the Rust harness:
69	 *
70	 *   EVENT name=FILL_COMPLETE held=32 secret=<hex>
71	 *   EVENT name=WRITE_BORROWS fd=N held=32
72	 *   EVENT name=CLOSE_STILL_HELD fd=N held=32
73	 *   EVENT name=WRITE_FAILED held=32
74	 *   EVENT name=ABORT_UNLINK fd=N held=32
75	 *   EVENT name=RANDOM_FAILED held=N sentinel=M
76	 *   EVENT name=WIPED held=N sentinel=M steps=K
77	 *   EVENT name=OUTPUT_AFTER_WIPE fd=N
78	 *   EVENT name=GUEST_EXIT rc=N
79	 *   FAIL reason=...
80	 *
81	 * Exit status: 0 = every property for the requested mode held; 1 = a
82	 * regression was observed (a FAIL line was printed); 2 = usage/environment
83	 * error. The guest's stdout/stderr go to the capture files named on the
84	 * command line, so the event stream on this program's stdout stays
85	 * parseable.
86	 *
87	 * Linux ptrace semantics relied on:
88	 *   - PTRACE_TRACEME from the forked child before exec;
89	 *   - PTRACE_GET_SYSCALL_INFO tells syscall-entry/exit stops apart even
90	 *     with a seccomp-trap SIGSYS interleaved (a trapped syscall has no exit
91	 *     stop: ENTRY -> SIGSYS delivery -> handler -> rt_sigreturn, after which
92	 *     the signal frame holds the emulated return value and PC);
93	 *   - PTRACE_SINGLESTEP runs one user instruction. On arm64 stepping over
94	 *     an svc first re-reports the syscall-entry stop (registers hold the
95	 *     call arguments, the syscall has not run) and then traps at the
96	 *     instruction following the svc with the return value in x0; on x86_64
97	 *     the single-step trap whose RIP is the syscall instruction is the
98	 *     before-execution observation point. Both are checked, so a
99	 *     stdout/stderr write is caught *before* it leaves the process.
100	 *
101	 * Supported architectures: x86_64 and aarch64 (the same set as the randtrap
102	 * shim). Compilation refuses anything else.
103	 */
